  "worked_solution": "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $…$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $…$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the probability mass function for  $Y$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ | $0.45$ |\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\\,{f}_{Y}({y}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =0+0.25+0.9 \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
